## **Abstract**

Artificial intelligence has achieved transformative results in domains defined by digital content. Yet the production of physical artifacts—the substrate of civilization—remains governed by slow, expert-constrained engineering workflows. This document establishes **Autonomous Physical Engineering (APE)** as a formal discipline: the science of transforming human intent into verified physical reality through closed-loop intelligent systems.

We propose **The Fundamental Law of Engineering Acceleration**: the time from intent to artifact is governed by four core capabilities—**Search**, **Verification**, **Execution**, and **Learning**—constrained by energy and institutional friction. We derive a dimensionally consistent equation with an explicit calibration constant, clarify the distinct roles of search efficiency ($V_{search}$) and verification confidence ($V$), and introduce energy ($E_{energy}$) and institutional friction ($F_{institutional}$) as first-class constraints on civilizational productivity. 

We present an expanded complexity index, a six-layer framework with specified feedback loops, a probabilistic graphical model for verification, an empirical validation protocol, and a five-year roadmap. We state explicit falsifiability criteria that transform this document from a theoretical framework into a testable, benchmarkable science.

