## **6. Execution and Energy**

### **6.1 Execution as a Capability ($E_{execution}$)**
Execution transforms verified designs into physical atoms. It is modulated by manufacturing automation (CNC, additive, robotics), process fidelity, and supply chain latency. It is explicitly represented in the denominator of the Fundamental Law.

### **6.2 Energy as a First-Class Constraint ($E_{energy}$)**
The largest hidden variable in APE is energy. Future systems consume massive power for GPU clusters, HPC simulations, and robotic manufacturing. 

$$ E_{energy} = \frac{\text{Energy consumed per artifact [kWh]}}{\text{Baseline energy per artifact [kWh]}} $$
*(Baseline: 1990-era CAD + manual manufacturing ≈ 500 kWh per ton of steel-equivalent).*

**APE Target:** $E_{energy} < 1.0$ via distilled models, physics-informed neural operators (faster simulation), and renewable-powered manufacturing.

### **6.3 The Energy-Geopolitics Connection**
Energy constraints connect APE directly to sustainability (carbon footprint), economics (energy is 20–40% of manufacturing cost), and geopolitics. Nations or communities that master low-energy APE gain a compounding advantage.

